WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.340
Both are called cyclones.

2
00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.546
Both spin around low pressure.

3
00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:06.461
But one is born over a warm

4
00:00:06.461 --> 00:00:09.074
sea, and the other where warm and

5
00:00:09.074 --> 00:00:10.535
cold air collide.

6
00:00:10.935 --> 00:00:12.816
A tropical cyclone needs a large sea

7
00:00:12.816 --> 00:00:15.904
surface warmer than twenty-seven degrees,

8
00:00:15.904 --> 00:00:17.715
the Coriolis force,

9
00:00:17.715 --> 00:00:19.190
steady winds with height,

10
00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.173
and a weak low to start from.

11
00:00:21.573 --> 00:00:23.106
At its centre is the eye,

12
00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:25.369
a calm patch of sinking air.

13
00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:26.890
Around it is the eye wall,

14
00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:29.443
where air spirals up to great heights.

15
00:00:29.842 --> 00:00:31.282
It draws its power from the warm

16
00:00:31.282 --> 00:00:32.489
sea.

17
00:00:32.489 --> 00:00:33.720
Once it crosses the coast,

18
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.844
called landfall, it is cut off from

19
00:00:35.844 --> 00:00:37.712
that fuel and weakens.

20
00:00:38.112 --> 00:00:40.678
Temperate cyclones form outside the tropics,

21
00:00:40.678 --> 00:00:42.350
along the polar front,

22
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.463
where warm and cold air meet.

23
00:00:44.463 --> 00:00:47.143
They have clear warm and cold fronts.

24
00:00:47.543 --> 00:00:49.946
Tropical cyclones are fiercer and move from

25
00:00:49.946 --> 00:00:51.096
east to west.

26
00:00:51.096 --> 00:00:53.673
Temperate ones cover a larger area,

27
00:00:53.673 --> 00:00:55.182
move from west to east,

28
00:00:55.182 --> 00:00:57.317
and can form over land too.

29
00:00:57.717 --> 00:00:58.704
For GS One,

30
00:00:58.704 --> 00:01:00.573
remember four contrasts:

31
00:01:00.573 --> 00:01:02.048
where they form,

32
00:01:02.048 --> 00:01:05.262
fronts, strength, and the direction they travel.

